  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nSo far, not really much fog on webcams. Strong low level flow across \nthe area will probably limit fog to upslope enhanced areas (western \nridges in Corson/Dewey, Leola hills and south side of the Sisseton \nhills). \n\nFor today, we have a broad area of high clouds moving overhead, \nwhile in the low levels there remains strong southerly flow and an \narea of low stratus coming up from the south. \n\nFor winds, the gradient remains around 12-14mb across the state, \nwith a surface low developing over Wyoming. 1/2km winds are running \naround 30 to 35kts in the east and 20 to 30kts in north central \nSouth Dakota. \n\nAs for temperatures, high clouds on the nighttime microphysics and \nrepresented in the RRFS are quite thick. Then we have the low \nstratus, and while only about 400ft thick in RRFS BUFKIT profiles, \nthe stratus, high clouds and low sun angle will all make it that \nmuch more difficult to warm the surface despite being under a fairly \nmild airmass. While 850mb temperatures are a standard deviation \nabove climo, the base of the inversion is up close to the 800mb \nlevel and 700mb temperatures are 2 standard deviations above climo. \nGiven the depth of the layer below the inversion and cloud cover it \nmay be difficult to really get into that very warm air. That said, \nwith mixing this morning, temperatures are still in the upper 30s to \nupper 40s. Will stick with NBM which is in the mid 50s. \n\nThe core of the mildest air moves overhead tonight. Deterministic \nRRFS and GFS have +12 to +14C at 850mb across north central South \nDakota. The gradient drops to the east to about +3 to +6C. 1/2km \nwinds likewise peak at close to 45kts across the Sisseton \nHills/Buffalo Ridge downslope areas. Still a fairly elevated \ninversion, with unidirectional shear within the profile, resulting \nin less efficient downslope enhancement. Peak wind within the \nprofile is mid/upper 40s (kts) so might get that area of I29 in \nDeuel county up around 50+ mph but otherwise not a widespread \ndownslope event. \n\nThe mild airmass lifts northeast as the surface low lifts into \nwestern North Dakota. Mixing will be better Wednesday in the warm \nsector, with RRFS sky cover showing a gap across much of the CWA. \nWe'll also be able to get a more widespread southwesterly component \nto the winds before the cold front comes through, with timing later \nin the day. The core of coolest air now punching along the ND/SD \nstate line. Mixed winds during the evening/overnight in the RRFS \nBUFKIT profiles are around 30-35kts while the more mixy GFS is well \ninto the 40s (kts). \n\nThe cooler airmass is actually still slightly above normal, \nwith the coolest timeframe temperatures Thursday sill running \nnear to as much as 10F above normal. That means the next system \ncoming in for Friday runs into what is still a seasonally mild \nairmass.\n\nIn regards to the Friday system, deterministic model timing and \ntracks are a little less consistent in comparison to 24 hours ago. \nAt 500mb its being depicted as an open wave as it moves into the \nNorthern Plains, with a series of weak southwest flow \nshortwaves. At the surface the low is being depicted as much \nless intense. Seeing a likewise trend down in PoPs and QPF. For \nexample, probabilities of 0.25\" in 24 hours have dropped to \n20-40% in comparison to the previous run of 25-50% and the 01Z \nNBM from 24 hours ago which topped out at 40-75%. Much of the \nwrap around moisture in the colder airmass (representing \npotential snow accumulations) has mostly evaporated.",
